    networkVariablesTitle:"04 · Variables and system structure",networkVariable1:"Individual involvement: passes made, received and the sum of both actions.",networkVariable2:"Directed relationships: passes from one player to another.",networkVariable3:"Pairwise relationships: attempted, completed and failed passes in each direction.",networkVariable4:"Spatial distribution: locations of pass starts and ends.",networkUniverseNote:"The pairwise table counts identifiable attempts; networks and maps use completed passes. These are different data universes.",
    networkResultsTitle:"05 · Results and audit",networkResultsIntro:"Summary of audited counts:",networkMetric:"Metric",networkEvents:"Events downloaded",networkPassesBefore:"Passes before filtering",networkValidPasses:"Valid passes used",networkPlayersAvailable:"Players available",networkPlayersSelected:"Players selected",networkEdgesBefore:"Directed connections before threshold (all players)",networkEdgesFiltered:"Filtered network connections",networkEdgesFull:"Full network connections",networkPassesFiltered:"Passes represented in filtered network",networkPassesFull:"Passes represented in full network",networkAuditNote:"The 94 and 91 connections are calculated across all players with valid passing involvement. Within the eight-player visual selection, the full network contains 49 and 39 directed connections; requiring at least three passes independently in each direction leaves 35 and 15.",networkParticipationIntro:"Players with the highest involvement:",networkPlayer:"Player",networkExecuted:"Made",networkReceived:"Received",networkTotal:"Involvements",networkParticipationNote:"The top four players for each team are shown; the technical report contains all eight selected players.",
    networkVisualsTitle:"06 · System visualisations",networkVisualsIntro:"Team outputs, with passing networks and spatial representations generated by the notebooks.",networkIvoryImageAlt:"Total Interaction System visualisations for Côte d’Ivoire.",networkIvoryCaption:"Côte d’Ivoire · Match 3923881 · StatsBomb Open Data",networkNigeriaImageAlt:"Total Interaction System visualisations for Nigeria.",networkNigeriaCaption:"Nigeria · Match 3923881 · StatsBomb Open Data",
    networkPairsTitle:"07 · Pairwise relationships",networkIvoryPairs:"Côte d’Ivoire: Konan - Adingra (38), Konan - Ndicka (36), Ndicka - Kossounou (35), and Konan - Seri (23). Konan–Adingra recorded 27 and 11 passes by direction; Ndicka–Kossounou, 18 and 17.",networkNigeriaPairs:"Nigeria: Bassey - Troost-Ekong (17), Ajayi - Troost-Ekong (14), Bassey - Sanusi (10), and Iwobi - Sanusi (9). Bassey–Troost-Ekong recorded 8 and 9 passes by direction.",
    networkInterpretationTitle:"08 · Football interpretation",networkInterpretationText:"Côte d’Ivoire completed 388 passes compared with Nigeria’s 189, and generated a broader network of relationships among the eight selected players. Across all players with valid passing involvement, the teams had a similar number of distinct directed connections before the threshold (94 and 91). Within the eight-player selection, the full networks contain 49 connections for Côte d’Ivoire and 39 for Nigeria. Applying a minimum of three passes independently to each direction leaves 35 and 15 connections, respectively. This points to a difference in the recurrence of relationships within the selected networks, not simply in the initial number of distinct connections. These connections account for 70.1% of Côte d’Ivoire’s passes used and 44.4% of Nigeria’s. For individual involvement, Konan (124) and Seri (115) stand out for Côte d’Ivoire, while Bassey (61) and Troost-Ekong (50) lead for Nigeria. Pairwise relationships add nuance: Konan - Adingra is asymmetric (27 and 11 by direction), while Ndicka - Kossounou is more balanced (18 and 17); Bassey - Troost-Ekong is also reciprocal (8 and 9). Overall, the data describe greater passing volume and recurring connections for Côte d’Ivoire, while fewer Nigerian relationships exceed the selected threshold.",networkInterpretationCaveat:"The interpretation is limited to passing volume, recurrence, individual involvement and reciprocity. It does not establish tactical superiority, territorial dominance, effective progression or decision quality. Pairwise counts refer to identifiable attempts, whereas networks and maps use completed passes; these are different data universes. Further analysis would need to calculate progression associated with each connection and examine the action after reception, alongside video and match-context review.",
    networkLimitationsTitle:"09 · Limitations and reading criteria",networkLimit1:"Individual involvement measures passing volume; it does not, by itself, equal tactical influence.",networkLimit2:"Connection frequency does not demonstrate decision quality or progression effectiveness.",networkLimit3:"Pairwise relationships count attempts; networks and maps use completed passes.",networkLimit4:"The maps do not describe off-ball occupation or the creation of advantages.",networkLimit5:"Tactical conclusions require video and match-context validation.",
